"07_streams.mst — the stream position protocol (DolphinDart DD9).

 `WriteStream` already TRACKS its position — the ivar is right there in
 `st/world/18_writestream.mst` — it simply never exposed it. ANSI and Dolphin
 both put `position` / `position:` on PositionableStream, and Dolphin's own
 `LayoutContext>>repositionSubViewsOf:` uses it as the count of placements
 accumulated so far:

     count := stream position.
     count == 0 ifTrue: [^self].

 Found by running that method: `does not understand position`, from inside a
 layout pass."
Object subclass: WriteStream [
    position [ ^position ]

    "Setting the position back is how Dolphin truncates a stream in place. It
     may only move WITHIN what has been written — seeking past the end would
     expose whatever the backing collection happens to hold there."
    position: anInteger [
        (anInteger < 0 or: [ anInteger > position ])
            ifTrue: [ ^self error: 'position out of range' ].
        position := anInteger
    ]

    "── growing must PRESERVE THE BACKING COLLECTION'S IDENTITY ──────────
     The world's `growTo:` allocates a replacement and rebinds `collection`,
     which is the usual Smalltalk implementation and is invisible to any code
     that reads the result through `contents`. Dolphin's is not such code:

         stream := WriteStream on: (newPlacements := Array new).
         ... stream nextPut: placement ...
         1 to: count do: [:i | (newPlacements at: i) deferWindowPos: hdwp]

     — `UI.LayoutContext>>repositionSubViewsOf:`, verbatim. It writes through
     the stream and reads back through its OWN reference to the collection, so
     a replacement leaves it holding the original empty Array and the loop
     fails with an index error. Dolphin grows arrays in place, so the two
     references stay the same object.

     An Array here is a Dart List, which grows in place too, so appending
     preserves identity exactly as Dolphin does. Only the Array case is
     overridden: a String-backed stream has no such caller and the world's
     bulk-copy growth is faster.

     Found by running Dolphin's layout: `RangeError (index): Valid value range
     is empty: 0`, from inside a WM_SIZE.

     The non-Array branch repeats the world's two lines rather than calling
     `super nextPut:`: this is a cross-file REOPEN of the same class, so
     `super` looks in the SUPERCLASS, not at the definition being replaced —
     the front-end says so plainly (`unsupported super send (not found in
     supers)`).

     The test is on the class NAME. `(Array new) isKindOf: Array` answers
     FALSE — Array is a bridged class whose instances are Dart Lists — so a
     kind test here silently never fires, which is how the first version of
     this method looked correct and changed nothing."
    nextPut: e [
        collection class name = 'Array'
            ifTrue: [
                position = collection size
                    ifTrue: [ Smalltalk arrayAppend: collection with: e ]
                    ifFalse: [ collection at: position + 1 put: e ] ]
            ifFalse: [
                position = collection size ifTrue: [ self grow ].
                collection at: position + 1 put: e ].
        position := position + 1.
        ^e
    ]
]
